Relevant columns & potential usage  
• NETHERLANDS_OPEN_MAP_DATA.NETHERLANDS.V_ROAD  
  – "CLASS": main road class → to group totals.  
  – "SUBCLASS": more‑granular class → to group totals.  
  – "LENGTH_M": text column that stores the length in meters → will be CAST to NUMBER and summed.  
  – "QUADKEY": Bing‑tiles string → filter on segments that start with ‘12020210’ (Amsterdam tile) and ‘12020211’ (Rotterdam tile).  
  – "GEO_CORDINATES": geography line → optional spatial join with city polygons if needed.  
• NETHERLANDS_OPEN_MAP_DATA.NETHERLANDS.V_ADMINISTRATIVE  
  – "ADMIN_LEVEL": ‘8’ ≈ municipality → helps fetch Amsterdam / Rotterdam polygon.  
  – "NAMES": VARIANT that contains multilingual / local names → flatten to find the Dutch name.  
  – "GEO_CORDINATES": geography polygon → spatial join with roads to validate which quadkey belongs to which city (optional).

Typical conditions we will use  
1. WHERE "QUADKEY" LIKE '12020210%'   -- Amsterdam tile.  
2. WHERE "QUADKEY" LIKE '12020211%'   -- Rotterdam tile.  
3. CAST("LENGTH_M" AS NUMBER) to aggregate numeric totals.  
4. WHERE "ADMIN_LEVEL" = '8' AND n.value ILIKE '%Amsterdam%' (or ‘%Rotterdam%’) after FLATTEN(NAMES).

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Below are 10 exploratory → increasingly complex Snowflake SQL queries (all limited to 20 rows when they output raw data) that gradually lead to the requested side‑by‑side comparison.  
All identifiers are enclosed in double quotes as required.

```sql
-- 1. Description: Inspect distinct road classes present in the whole dataset.
